# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DEV (durin-seli-dev) — non-secret, per-environment function config.
#
# Firebase loads `functions/.env.<PROJECT_ID>` at DEPLOY time and injects every
# key here into `process.env` for ALL deployed functions (not just `api`), so it
# reaches the Firestore/auth/scheduled email triggers too — unlike `runWith({
# secrets })`, which has to be bound per function.
#
# Loaded ONLY when deploying to `durin-seli-dev` (i.e. `--project dev`), so dev
# and prod can never pick up each other's values.
#
# COMMITTED ON PURPOSE — nothing in here is a secret. Real secrets stay in Cloud
# Secret Manager (`firebase functions:secrets:set <NAME>`); `.env.local` /
# `.env.dev.local` remain emulator-only and git-ignored.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Portal base URL used to build every link in transactional email — the footer
# link plus the auth deep-links (`/reset-password`, `/verify-email`, `/claim`,
# `/login?magic=1`), agent invites (`/create-account?invite=…`), the guest
# consultation Paystack return (`/a/<slug>/confirm`) and client payment returns.
#
# MUST point at the portal wired to THIS Firebase project: the `oobCode` in reset
# / verify / claim links is project-scoped, so a prod URL here produces links that
# simply won't validate against dev. Without this file the code falls back to the
# prod default (https://portal.weareseli.com) — which is exactly the bug this
# fixes: dev emails linking users into production.
APP_URL=https://japa-portal-dev--durin-seli-dev.us-central1.hosted.app
